  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-01-02T06:28:19.354Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Forward:  Currently, Quest avatars do not allow sounds to be attached due to peculiarities in how the Quest handles sound. The traditional spacial audio methods used by VRChat ultimately result in application misbehavior when a large number of audio sources are present.   However, this is NOT to say the Quest cannot handle avatar audio. Rather, similar to shaders, VRChat will need to intelligent manage audio, replacing certain aspects as necessary. \n— \n \nProposal: \n \n(1.) VRCSDK for Quest shall convert all instances of \"Audio Source\" with a custom \"VRCSDK Mixed Audio Source\". \n \n(2.) Behavior of the mixed audio source shall go as follows:  \n    (a.) Mixed audio sources, upon activation, shall NOT play audio from itself (with exception), but rather MIX audio, from source, into Parent-User's speech output, client side. \n    (b.) Mixed audio shall take the VRCSDK Spatial Audio Source configuration into account, adjusting the gain of the audio source to be equivalent to that of which the parent-user would hear from the head of the avatar. \n        {e.g.} If the Parent-User hears audio source at 70% volume on spawn, then audio source shall be mixed at a ratio of 0.7:1 audio to speech. \n    (c.) Only exception for [a.] shall be when the Parent-User is also the Client-User, at which point, audio shall be played from a dummy audio source near the head, client-side. \n \n(3.) Animations referencing audio sources themselves (as opposed to game objects) shall be intelligently re-mapped at build time to converted VRCSDK Mixed Audio Source. \n \n(4.) Parent-Users which have been muted client-side by Client-User shall also respect this status for VRC Mixed Audio Sources. \n \n(5.) To provide better portability and speed, atop existing unity features to downmix audio files to mono, and compress them via different file formats, per-platform, the VRCSDK may also add the OPTION for bitrate reduction, converting audio from higher bitrates, down to a more compact and reasonable format, when necessary. – Alternatively, a hard bitrate and sample rate (Hz) may be enforced. \n— \nThrough this proposal, Quest users would be able to experience avatar audio in a simple and effective way, that relies on *existing* spatial audio processing used by existing systems, with [hopefully] minimal overhead of mixing audio into speech.",
